<header><p class="st-kicker">principality · Caucasus</p><h1 class="st-title">Principality of Temirgoy</h1><p class="st-meta">1500 CE – 1864 CE (approx.)</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A princely Circassian tribe on the Laba and Belaya rivers, whose dialect became the basis of the modern Adyghe literary language.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The Temirgoy, or Kemirgoy, were one of the aristocratic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/circassians">Adyghe</a> tribes of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/circassia">Circassia</a>, living along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/laba-river">Laba</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belaya-river-kuban">Belaya</a> rivers. Their princely house, the Bolotoko, rose to prominence among the western Circassians by the 16th and 17th centuries. Earlier history is not recorded separately.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Society was ranked into princes, nobles, free peasants and serfs, bound by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/circassians">Adyghe</a> code of honour. The Temirgoy were among the strongest western tribes and often led coalitions against the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/crimean-khanate">Crimean Khanate</a> and later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>. Prince Jambulat Bolotoko led raids and negotiations with Russia in the early 19th century.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> gradually forced the Temirgoy princes into submission, and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/russian-empire">Russian Empire</a> completed its conquest of their land in 1864. Many emigrated to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a>, but some remained in what became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adygea">Adygea</a>, and their dialect underlies literary <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/circassians">Adyghe</a>.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="principality, 1500 CE – 1864 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M20 74 H80 V60 H20 Z"/><path d="M20 60 Q35 46 50 60 Q65 46 80 60"/><circle cx="20" cy="54" r="3"/><circle cx="35" cy="48" r="3"/><circle cx="50" cy="44" r="3"/><circle cx="65" cy="48" r="3"/><circle cx="80" cy="54" r="3"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">principality</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Caucasus</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:89.55%;width:7.24%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1500 CE – 1864 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>principality</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Jambulat Bolotoko</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Adyghe</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/russia">Russia</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Temirgoy, Kemirgoy, Chemguy</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1682&amp;focus=principality-of-temirgoy" aria-label="Principality of Temirgoy on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5616 193 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Principality of Temirgoy lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5955 888h0M6487 457h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5936 449h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M5936 449h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day Russia. <a href="/atlas?year=1682&amp;focus=principality-of-temirgoy">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

